ad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gitte Miller","key":"balslevpau","last":"Balslev","locid":"ce6bf86c-6ed8-4ba8-a4bb-14f673c189f9","name":"Balslev, Gitte Miller","orcid":"0000-0002-3586-2343","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-424e0ed7-c472-48ec-bd2f-8628d4a1a0a0"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The Ethics of Creativity in AI Education","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-802006","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Sorting networks are sorting algorithms that execute a sequence of operations independently of the input. Since they can be implemented directly as circuits, sorting networks are easy to implement in hardware – but they are also used often in software to improve performance of base cases of standard recursive sorting algorithms. For this purpose, they are translated into machine-code instructions in a systematic way. Recently, a deep-learning system discovered better implementations than previously known of some sorting networks with up to 8 inputs. In this article, we show that all these examples are instances of a general pattern whereby some instructions are removed. We show that this removal can be done when a particular set of constraints on integers is satisfiable, and identify conditions where we can reduce this problem to propositional satisfiability. We systematically apply this general construction to improve the best-known implementations of sorting networks of size up to 128, which are the ones most commonly found in software implementations.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Schneider-Kamp, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-783001"],"event":{"end":"2024-05-31","place":"Port Louis, mu","start":"2024-05-26","title":"25th Conference on Logic for Programming, Artificial Intelligence and Reasoning, LPAR 2024","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís"],"keyword":["Satisfiability","Sorting networks","program optimization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"minimizing sorting networks at the sub comparator level","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85207836710","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-783001","identifier":[{"id":"85207836710","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-3e5bd2a9-a183-4360-a97a-7fee97467965"],"keyword":["constraint solving","program optimization","satisfiability","sorting networks"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-303A224D11918493B3157C7B61CDEE88"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages","SDU, Faculty of Science, SDU Climate Cluster"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"schneiderkampau","last":"Schneider-Kam","locid":"5a2afe33-7a0e-414d-b352-b4b366c08af8","name":"Schneider-Kamp, Peter","name_org":"Schneider-Kam, Peter","orcid":"0000-0003-4000-5570","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.29007/lzks","issn":["23987340"],"pages":"36-50","publisher":"EasyChair Publications","series":"EPiC Series in Computing","status":"p","subtitle":"Artificial Intelligence and Reasoning","title":"Proceedings of 25th Conference on Logic for Programming"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85207836710"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3e5bd2a9-a183-4360-a97a-7fee97467965"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Minimizing Sorting Networks at the Sub-Comparator Level","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-783001","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Lead generation refers to the identification of potential topics (the ‘leads’) of importance for journalists to report on. In this paper we present a new lead generation tool based on a microservice architecture, which includes a component of explainable AI. The lead generation tool collects and stores historical and real-time data from a web source, like Google Trends, and generates current and future leads. These leads are produced by an engine for hypothetical reasoning based on logical rules, which is a novel implementation of a recent theory. Finally, the leads are displayed on a web interface for end users, in particular journalists. This interface provides information on why a specific topic is or may become a lead, assisting journalists in deciding where to focus their attention. We carry out an empirical evaluation of the performance of our tool.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Kostopoulou, Sofia","Montesi, Fabrizio","Vistrup, Jonas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-723767"],"event":{"end":"2023-10-25","place":"Larnaca, cy","start":"2023-10-24","title":"10th IFIP WG 6.12 European Conference on Service-Oriented and Cloud Computing, ESOCC 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Vistrup, Jonas"],"keyword":["Explainable AI","Lead generation","Microservices"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"μ xl explainable lead generation with microservices and hypothetical answers","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/252667226/Lead_Generation_paper_accepted.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Work partially supported by Villum Fonden, grants no. 29518 and 50079, and the Independent Research Fund Denmark, grant no. 0135-00219."],"id":"S01-000.00-723767","identifier":[{"id":"85175827095","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-f2d461cc-2160-444c-9139-e5f54cd60897"],"keyword":["Explainable AI","Lead generation","Microservices"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FC1FE4CD34A29E4B1764A69B07EE4E8F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/252667226/Lead_Generation_paper_accepted.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sofia","key":"kostopouloupau","last":"Kostopoulou","locid":"fd6c9471-6ddd-4538-b203-0a46a76aa5ae","name":"Kostopoulou, Sofia","orcid":"0000-0002-5557-1295","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas","key":"vistruppau","last":"Vistrup","locid":"59f038f5-55e6-4339-bd05-1aa06b08971c","name":"Vistrup, Jonas","orcid":"0000-0001-7704-3656","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"George A.","key":"papadopoulospedh","last":"Papadopoulos","name":"Papadopoulos, George A.","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Florian","key":"rademacherpedh","last":"Rademacher","name":"Rademacher, Florian","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jacopo","key":"soldanipedh","last":"Soldani","name":"Soldani, Jacopo","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-46235-1_1","isbn":["9783031462344"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"3-18","publisher":"Springer Science+Business Media","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"Proceedings of IFIP WG 6.12 European Conference","title":"Service-Oriented and Cloud Computing -"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175827095"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-f2d461cc-2160-444c-9139-e5f54cd60897"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"μ XL : Explainable Lead Generation with Microservices and Hypothetical Answers","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-723767","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Choreographic programming is a paradigm where programmers write global descriptions of distributed protocols, called choreographies, and correct implementations are automatically generated by a mechanism called projection. Not all choreographies are projectable, because decisions made by one process must be communicated to other processes whose behaviour depends on them – a property known as knowledge of choice. The standard formulation of knowledge of choice disallows protocols such as third-party authentication with retries, where two processes iteratively interact, and other processes wait to be notified at the end of this loop. In this work we show how knowledge of choice can be weakened, extending the class of projectable choreographies with these and other interesting behaviours. The whole development is formalised in Coq. Working with a proof assistant was crucial to our development, because of the help it provided with detecting counterintuitive edge cases that would otherwise have gone unnoticed.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Rasmussen, Robert R.","Montesi, Fabrizio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-680411"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís"],"keyword":["Certified implementation","Choreographic Programming","Distributed protocols","Theorem proving"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"keep me out of the loop a more flexible choreographic projection","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.29007/wbw3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-680411","identifier":[{"id":"85166488052","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-7a0c5776-0522-4e40-bf0f-b68160ddab36"],"keyword":["Certified implementation","Choreographic Programming","Distributed protocols","Theorem proving"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-65F49A574EFB2C641D99C60D13BD7386"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.29007/wbw3","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert R.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"bff98834-dcf3-44a9-978d-513f6f61b2af","name":"Rasmussen, Robert R.","orcid":"0009-0009-0714-0544","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ruzica","key":"piskacpedh","last":"Piskac","name":"Piskac, Ruzica","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andrei","key":"voronkovpedh","last":"Voronkov","name":"Voronkov, Andrei","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.29007/wbw3","pages":"144-163","publisher":"Ethnographic Praxis in Industry Conference (EPIC)","status":"p","subtitle":"EPiC Series in Computing","title":"Proceedings of 24th International Conference on Logic for Programming, Artificial Intelligence and Reasoning","volume":"94"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85166488052"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-7a0c5776-0522-4e40-bf0f-b68160ddab36"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Keep me out of the loop: a more flexible choreographic projection","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-680411","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Choreographic programming is a paradigm for writing coordination plans for distributed systems from a global point of view, from which correct-by-construction decentralised implementations can be generated automatically. Theory of choreographies typically includes a number of complex results that are proved by structural induction. The high number of cases and the subtle details in some of these proofs has led to important errors being found in published works. In this work, we formalise the theory of a choreographic programming language in Coq. Our development includes the basic properties of this language, a proof of its Turing completeness, a compilation procedure to a process language, and an operational characterisation of the correctness of this procedure. Our formalisation experience illustrates the benefits of using a theorem prover: we get both an additional degree of confidence from the mechanised proof, and a significant simplification of the underlying theory. Our results offer a foundation for the future formal development of choreographic languages.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Montesi, Fabrizio","Peressotti, Marco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-685317"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís"],"keyword":["Choreographic Programming","Concurrency","Process calculi","Theorem proving"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a formal theory of choreographic programming","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10817-023-09665-3","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors would like to thank the anonymous reviewers for their comments and suggestions, which contributed to increasing the quality of this article. This work was partially supported by Villum Fonden, grant nr 29518, and the Independent Research Fund Denmark, grant nr 0135-00219."],"id":"S01-000.00-685317","identifier":[{"id":"85160450051","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-a84ae689-7b29-4162-a968-ba2e15eb50a5"],"keyword":["Choreographic programming","Concurrency","Process calculi","Theorem proving"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-26BA734B3CCA1CBC895BB463023AE3B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10817-023-09665-3","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/234057913/s10817_023_09665_3.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages","SDU, Faculty of Science, SDU Climate Cluster"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"peressottipau","last":"Peressotti","locid":"afca1964-ceda-46fa-b7c7-f6e77e401024","name":"Peressotti, Marco","orcid":"0000-0002-0243-0480","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10817-023-09665-3","issn":["01687433","15730670"],"issn_org":["01687433"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Automated Reasoning","paperid":"21","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Automated Reasoning","volume":"67"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160450051"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-a84ae689-7b29-4162-a968-ba2e15eb50a5"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"A Formal Theory of Choreographic Programming","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-685317","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Choreographic programming is a paradigm where a concurrent or distributed system is developed in a top-down fashion. Programs, called choreographies, detail the desired interactions between processes, and can be compiled to distributed implementations based on message passing. Choreographic languages usually guarantee deadlock-freedom and provide an operational correspondence between choreographies and their compiled implementations, but until now little work has been done on verifying other properties. This paper presents a Hoare-style logic for reasoning about the behaviour of choreographies, and illustrate its usage in representative examples. We show that this logic is sound and complete, and discuss decidability of its judgements. Using existing results from choreographic programming, we show that any functional correctness property proven for a choreography also holds for its compiled implementation.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Graversen, Eva","Montesi, Fabrizio","Peressotti, Marco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-685157"],"event":{"end":"2023-06-23","place":"Lisbon, pt","start":"2023-06-19","title":"25th IFIP WG 6.1 International Conference on Coordination Models and Language, COORDINATION 2023, held as part of the 18th International Federated Conference on Distributed Computing Techniques, DisCoTec 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Graversen, Eva"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reasoning about choreographic programs","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.14539","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was partially supported by Villum Fonden, grant nr 29518."],"id":"S01-000.00-685157","identifier":[{"id":"85164736949","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-6838fcc8-43e1-49ef-a5f5-614477820609"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4471DABAA0D3A49DBBF430AA90EDA03F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.14539","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages","SDU, Faculty of Science, SDU Climate Cluster"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Eva","key":"graversenpau","last":"Graversen","locid":"1a2ccb3c-832d-46ea-8d84-6f1e0f0da3dc","name":"Graversen, Eva","orcid":"0000-0002-9430-4907","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"peressottipau","last":"Peressotti","locid":"afca1964-ceda-46fa-b7c7-f6e77e401024","name":"Peressotti, Marco","orcid":"0000-0002-0243-0480","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sung-Shik","key":"jongmanspedh","last":"Jongmans","name":"Jongmans, Sung-Shik","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Antónia","key":"lopespedh","last":"Lopes","name":"Lopes, Antónia","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-35361-1_8","isbn":["9783031353604"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"144-162","publisher":"Springer Science+Business Media","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Coordination Models and Languages - 25th IFIP WG 6.1 International Conference, COORDINATION 2023, Held as Part of the 18th International Federated Conference on Distributed Computing Techniques, DisCoTec 2023, Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164736949"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-6838fcc8-43e1-49ef-a5f5-614477820609"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Reasoning About Choreographic Programs","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-685157","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Programming communicating processes is challenging, because it requires writing separate programs that perform compatible send and receive actions at the right time during execution. Leaving this task to the programmer can easily lead to bugs. Choreographic programming addresses this challenge by equipping developers with high-level abstractions for codifying the desired communication structures from a global viewpoint. Given a choreography, implementations of the involved processes can be automatically generated by endpoint projection (EPP). While choreographic programming prevents manual mistakes in the implementation of communications, the correctness of a choreographic programming framework crucially hinges on the correctness of its complex compiler, which has motivated formalisation of theories of choreographic programming in theorem provers. In this paper, we build upon one of these formalisations to construct a toolchain that produces executable code from a choreography.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Lugović, Lovro","Montesi, Fabrizio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-685191"],"event":{"end":"2023-06-23","place":"Lisbon, pt","start":"2023-06-19","title":"43rd IFIP WG 6.1 International Conference on Formal Techniques for Distributed Objects, Components, and Systems, FORTE 2023, held as part of the 18th International Federated Conference on Distributed Computing Techniques, DisCoTec 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Lugović, Lovro"],"keyword":["Choreographic Programming","Formal verification","Jolie"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"certified compilation of choreographies with hacc","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.03972.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was partially supported by Villum Fonden, grant no. 29518, and the Independent Research Fund Denmark, grant no. 0135-00219."],"id":"S01-000.00-685191","identifier":[{"id":"85164267408","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-77bf66d3-8e09-485d-9aec-b61ffbbc71d6"],"keyword":["Certified compilation","Choreographic programming","Formal verification","Jolie"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2AF6ED22BF6597096E9DC6701145ECEB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.03972.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity and Programming Languages","SDU, Faculty of Science, SDU Climate Cluster"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luís","key":"cruzfilipepau","last":"Cruz-Filipe","locid":"44dd7cea-a613-4dfd-9c6d-ac91a0729aad","name":"Cruz-Filipe, Luís","orcid":"0000-0002-7866-7484","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lovro","key":"lugovićpau","last":"Lugović","name":"Lugović, Lovro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marieke","key":"huismanpedh","last":"Huisman","name":"Huisman, Marieke","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"António","key":"ravarapedh","last":"Ravara","name":"Ravara, António","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-35355-0_3","isbn":["9783031353543"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"29-36","publisher":"Springer Science+Business Media","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Formal Techniques for Distributed Objects, Components, and Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164267408"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-77bf66d3-8e09-485d-9aec-b61ffbbc71d6"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Certified Compilation of Choreographies with hacc","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-685191","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We present Modular Choreographies, a new choreographic programming language that features modular functions. Modular Choreographies is aimed at simplicity: its communication abstraction follows the simple tradition from the “Alice and Bob” notation. We develop a compiler toolchain that translates choreographies into modular Java libraries, which developers can use to participate correctly in choreographies. The key novelty is to compile through the Choral language, which was previously proposed to define object-oriented choreographies: our toolchain compiles Modular Choreographies to Choral, and then leverages the existing Choral compiler to generate Java code. Our work is the first to bridge the simplicity of traditional choreographic programming languages with the requirement of generating modular libraries in a mainstream language (Java).","author_count":11,"contributors":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís","Madsen, Anne","Montesi, Fabrizio","Peressotti, Marco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-722960"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Cruz-Filipe, Luís"],"keyword":["Choreographic Programming","Choreographies","Modularity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modular choreographies bridging alice and bob notation to java","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/OASIcs.Microservices.2020-2022.3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-722960","identifier":[{"id":"85181987163","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-21767e37-3aec-46cd-b97d-0fa890dd16f9"],"keyword":["Choreographic Programming","Choreographies","Modularity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"